{"nodes": [{"id": "lesson_0001_sound_change", "label": "Lesson 0001: Sound Change and Reconstruction", "file_type": "document", "source_file": "teaching/oe-grammar/lessons/0001-sound-change-and-reconstruction.html", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "One mental model: broad inherited sound changes create Proto-Germanic; later branch-specific changes shape Old English and its relatives; reconstruction works because those outcomes are regular enough to compare."}, {"id": "proto_indo_european", "label": "Proto-Indo-European", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "teaching/oe-grammar/lessons/0001-sound-change-and-reconstruction.html", "source_location": "1. The Big Picture", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Earlier ancestor, reconstructed rather than directly attested. Late PIE is placed roughly 3000\u20132500 BCE, with a mobile accent and the older stop system."}, {"id": "proto_germanic", "label": "Proto-Germanic", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "teaching/oe-grammar/lessons/0001-sound-change-and-reconstruction.html", "source_location": "1a. Branching Sketch", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Shared Germanic stage after Grimm's Law and Verner's Law. Prehistoric, from the early 1st millennium BCE into the early centuries CE; common morphology and phonology continue after the big consonant shifts."}, {"id": "west_germanic", "label": "West Germanic", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "teaching/oe-grammar/lessons/0001-sound-change-and-reconstruction.html", "source_location": "1a. Branching Sketch", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "The subgroup on the path from Proto-Germanic through Northwest Germanic toward Pre-Old English and Old English."}, {"id": "grimms_law", "label": "Grimm's Law", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "teaching/oe-grammar/lessons/0001-sound-change-and-reconstruction.html", "source_location": "2. How the Famous Laws Fit Together", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "The big consonant shift from PIE into Proto-Germanic: voiceless stops become fricatives, voiced stops become voiceless stops, and voiced aspirates become voiced fricatives or stops. It is a Germanic innovation only, not a law of Indo-European as a whole. It must precede Verner's Law because Verner voices fricatives that Grimm created. Ultimate physical cause is treated as obscure."}, {"id": "verners_law", "label": "Verner's Law", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "teaching/oe-grammar/lessons/0001-sound-change-and-reconstruction.html", "source_location": "2. How the Famous Laws Fit Together", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Explains apparent exceptions to Grimm's Law. If the inherited accent was not on the syllable immediately before a Grimm-law fricative, that fricative could become voiced. It depends on the older PIE accent still mattering, and it precedes Germanic fixed initial stress."}, {"id": "i_mutation", "label": "i-mutation (i-umlaut)", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "teaching/oe-grammar/lessons/0001-sound-change-and-reconstruction.html", "source_location": "2. How the Famous Laws Fit Together", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Later branch-level vowel change triggered by a following i or j, often in an ending that later disappeared. Visible in mann ~ menn and f\u014dt ~ f\u0113t. It is not a rival to Grimm or Verner."}, {"id": "palatalization", "label": "Palatalization", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "teaching/oe-grammar/lessons/0001-sound-change-and-reconstruction.html", "source_location": "3c. Old English c/g Palatalization", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "A consonant is pulled forward toward a palatal or y-like pronunciation, often near front vowels or j. Old English makes this especially visible: c can be k-like or ch-like, g can be velar or y-like. Old Norse skip keeps velar sk where OE scip palatalizes toward ship."}, {"id": "dotted_c_and_g", "label": "Dotted \u010b and \u0121", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "teaching/oe-grammar/lessons/0001-sound-change-and-reconstruction.html", "source_location": "3c. Old English c/g Palatalization", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Modern editors write dotted \u010b and \u0121 not because manuscripts consistently did so, but so learners can keep palatal values separate from velar ones. The repository's Old English tooling treats those dots as worth preserving."}, {"id": "germanic_fixed_initial_stress", "label": "Germanic fixed initial stress", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "teaching/oe-grammar/lessons/0001-sound-change-and-reconstruction.html", "source_location": "2b. Why This Order?", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "The shift from PIE mobile accent to Germanic fixed initial stress happened during prehistoric Proto-Germanic, after the stage needed for Verner's Law and before the breakup into the earliest attested Germanic branches. The relative order is much clearer than any calendar date."}, {"id": "pie_mobile_accent", "label": "PIE mobile accent", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "teaching/oe-grammar/lessons/0001-sound-change-and-reconstruction.html", "source_location": "2d. How Did Accent Work in PIE?", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "PIE had a mobile, pitch-based accent, not later Germanic fixed-initial stress. Verner's Law needs that older placement: f\u00e6der shows d where a Grimm-only expectation would suggest \u00fe because the accent was not on the preceding syllable."}, {"id": "pie_stop_system", "label": "PIE three-way stop system", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "teaching/oe-grammar/lessons/0001-sound-change-and-reconstruction.html", "source_location": "2c. The Major PIE Stop Consonants", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Traditional Grimm's Law presentation compares classes, not isolated letters: voiceless stops p t k k\u02b7, voiced stops b d g g\u02b7, and voiced aspirates b\u02b0 d\u02b0 g\u02b0 g\u02b7\u02b0, plus s. Lehmann's inventory is the cited source."}, {"id": "oe_fot_etymology", "label": "OE f\u014dt walk-back", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "teaching/oe-grammar/lessons/0001-sound-change-and-reconstruction.html", "source_location": "4a. Worked Walk-Backs from Old English", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "PIE *p\u014dd- becomes a *f\u014dt- type by Grimm (p\u2192f, d\u2192t) with inherited o-grade. Plural f\u0113t reflects later i-mutation from a *f\u014dtiz-type plural whose i-ending was then lost. Verner's Law, breaking, gemination, and contraction are not needed."}, {"id": "oe_faeder_etymology", "label": "OE f\u00e6der walk-back", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "teaching/oe-grammar/lessons/0001-sound-change-and-reconstruction.html", "source_location": "4a. Worked Walk-Backs from Old English", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "PIE *p\u0259t\u1e17r first becomes a *fa\u00fe\u0113r type by Grimm (p\u2192f, t\u2192\u00fe). The accent on -t\u1e17r means the fricative was not immediately preceded by the inherited accent, so Verner voices \u00fe to the d of Gothic fadar and OE f\u00e6der. Fixed initial stress is later and is not what triggers Verner."}, {"id": "oe_teon_etymology", "label": "OE t\u0113on walk-back", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "teaching/oe-grammar/lessons/0001-sound-change-and-reconstruction.html", "source_location": "4a. Worked Walk-Backs from Old English", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "PIE *deuk- yields a *teuhan- type by Grimm (d\u2192t). Germanic eu becomes OE \u0113o, then intervocalic h is lost and the vowels contract to t\u0113on, with compensatory lengthening. The paradigm t\u0113ah : tugon : togen shows h~g alternation. The infinitive step is not breaking, gemination, or ordinary syncope."}, {"id": "vowel_contraction", "label": "Old English vowel contraction", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "teaching/oe-grammar/lessons/0001-sound-change-and-reconstruction.html", "source_location": "4a. OE t\u0113on", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Loss of intervocalic h creates a vowel sequence, and contraction yields a long diphthong. Length belongs to the contracted result (compensatory lengthening), not to an already-long first vowel. Wright groups t\u0113on with sl\u0113an from *sleahan."}, {"id": "oe_dialects_late_layer", "label": "Old English dialects as a late layer", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "teaching/oe-grammar/lessons/0001-sound-change-and-reconstruction.html", "source_location": "1b. Where the Old English Dialects Fit", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "West Saxon, Mercian, Northumbrian, and Kentish are dialects inside Old English after PIE \u2192 Proto-Germanic \u2192 West Germanic \u2192 Pre-Old English. They are not rivals to Grimm's Law or Verner's Law. Some changes spread unevenly across them."}, {"id": "wright_md_extract", "label": "data/sources/wright.md", "file_type": "document", "source_file": "teaching/oe-grammar/lessons/0001-sound-change-and-reconstruction.html", "source_location": "Sources Used", "source_url": "/Users/cmalek/src/workspace/wyrdcraeft/data/sources/wright.md", "captured_at": null, "author": "Joseph Wright and Elizabeth Mary Wright", "contributor": null, "rationale": "Workspace copy of Old English Grammar cited for vowel contraction, strong verbs, consonant alternations, dialect section \u00a7 3, and pronunciation."}, {"id": "lehmann_pgmc_introduction", "label": "Lehmann, Proto-Germanic introduction", "file_type": "paper", "source_file": "teaching/oe-grammar/lessons/0001-sound-change-and-reconstruction.html", "source_location": "Sources Used", "source_url": "https://lrc.la.utexas.edu/books/pgmc/1-introduction", "captured_at": null, "author": "Winfred P. Lehmann", "contributor": null, "rationale": "Primary lesson source for the comparative method, internal reconstruction, residues, and early Proto-Germanic pitch accent versus later fixed stress."}, {"id": "lehmann_pgmc_phonology", "label": "Lehmann, Proto-Germanic phonology", "file_type": "paper", "source_file": "teaching/oe-grammar/lessons/0001-sound-change-and-reconstruction.html", "source_location": "Sources Used", "source_url": "https://lrc.la.utexas.edu/books/pgmc/2-phonology", "captured_at": null, "author": "Winfred P. Lehmann", "contributor": null, "rationale": "Compact statement of Grimm's Law and related consonant developments. Also the source for the claim that no explanation of the first consonant shift has been widely accepted."}, {"id": "lehmann_pie_phonemics", "label": "Lehmann, Proto-Indo-European Phonology, PIE phonemics", "file_type": "paper", "source_file": "teaching/oe-grammar/lessons/0001-sound-change-and-reconstruction.html", "source_location": "Sources Used", "source_url": "https://lrc.la.utexas.edu/books/piep/13-pie-phonemics", "captured_at": null, "author": "Winfred P. Lehmann", "contributor": null, "rationale": "Cited for the PIE obstruent inventory used to teach the three stop classes."}, {"id": "lehmann_pie_development", "label": "Lehmann, Proto-Indo-European Phonology, development", "file_type": "paper", "source_file": "teaching/oe-grammar/lessons/0001-sound-change-and-reconstruction.html", "source_location": "Sources Used", "source_url": "https://lrc.la.utexas.edu/books/piep/15-development", "captured_at": null, "author": "Winfred P. Lehmann", "contributor": null, "rationale": "Cited for pitch accent in relation to vowel alternations and accent shifts."}, {"id": "ie_lexicon_foot", "label": "IE Lexicon entry: foot", "file_type": "paper", "source_file": "teaching/oe-grammar/lessons/0001-sound-change-and-reconstruction.html", "source_location": "Sources Used", "source_url": "https://lrc.la.utexas.edu/lex/master/1458", "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Gives PIE *p\u0113\u0306d-, *p\u014d\u0306d- with reflexes including OE f\u014dt and Latin pes."}, {"id": "ie_lexicon_father", "label": "IE Lexicon entry: father", "file_type": "paper", "source_file": "teaching/oe-grammar/lessons/0001-sound-change-and-reconstruction.html", "source_location": "Sources Used", "source_url": "https://lrc.la.utexas.edu/lex/master/1521", "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Gives PIE p\u0259t\u1e17(r), genitive p\u0259tr-\u00e9s, with OE f\u00e6der, Gothic fadar, Latin pater, and Sanskrit pit\u00e1r-."}, {"id": "ie_lexicon_draw", "label": "IE Lexicon entry: draw / tug", "file_type": "paper", "source_file": "teaching/oe-grammar/lessons/0001-sound-change-and-reconstruction.html", "source_location": "Sources Used", "source_url": "https://lrc.la.utexas.edu/lex/master/0355", "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Gives PIE *deuk- and lists OE t\u0113on, t\u0113ah, tugon, togen, Gothic tiuhan, and Latin d\u016bc\u014d."}, {"id": "old_norse_online_palatalization", "label": "Old Norse Online palatalization contrast", "file_type": "paper", "source_file": "teaching/oe-grammar/lessons/0001-sound-change-and-reconstruction.html", "source_location": "3d. Is This Just Old English?", "source_url": "https://lrc.la.utexas.edu/eieol/oruol/10", "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": "University of Texas LRC", "rationale": "Cited for the contrast that Old Norse skip keeps velar sk before i while Old English scip shows the palatalized development toward ship."}], "edges": [{"source": "lesson_0001_sound_change", "target": "oe_historical_grammar_course", "relation": "part_of", "confidence": "INFERRED", "confidence_score": 0.95, "source_file": "teaching/oe-grammar/lessons/0001-sound-change-and-reconstruction.html", "source_location": null, "weight": 0.95}, {"source": "lesson_0001_sound_change", "target": "proto_indo_european", "relation": "defines", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1.0, "source_file": "teaching/oe-grammar/lessons/0001-sound-change-and-reconstruction.html", "source_location": "1. The Big Picture", "weight": 1.0}, {"source": "lesson_0001_sound_change", "target": "proto_germanic", "relation": "defines", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1.0, "source_file": "teaching/oe-grammar/lessons/0001-sound-change-and-reconstruction.html", "source_location": "1a. Branching Sketch", "weight": 1.0}, {"source": "lesson_0001_sound_change", "target": "west_germanic", "relation": "defines", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1.0, "source_file": "teaching/oe-grammar/lessons/0001-sound-change-and-reconstruction.html", "source_location": "1a. Branching Sketch", "weight": 1.0}, {"source": "lesson_0001_sound_change", "target": "grimms_law", "relation": "defines", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1.0, "source_file": "teaching/oe-grammar/lessons/0001-sound-change-and-reconstruction.html", "source_location": "2. How the Famous Laws Fit Together", "weight": 1.0}, {"source": "lesson_0001_sound_change", "target": "verners_law", "relation": "defines", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1.0, "source_file": "teaching/oe-grammar/lessons/0001-sound-change-and-reconstruction.html", "source_location": "2. How the Famous Laws Fit Together", "weight": 1.0}, {"source": "lesson_0001_sound_change", "target": "i_mutation", "relation": "defines", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1.0, "source_file": "teaching/oe-grammar/lessons/0001-sound-change-and-reconstruction.html", "source_location": "2. How the Famous Laws Fit Together", "weight": 1.0}, {"source": "lesson_0001_sound_change", "target": "germanic_fixed_initial_stress", "relation": "defines", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1.0, "source_file": "teaching/oe-grammar/lessons/0001-sound-change-and-reconstruction.html", "source_location": "2b. Why This Order?", "weight": 1.0}, {"source": "lesson_0001_sound_change", "target": "pie_mobile_accent", "relation": "defines", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1.0, "source_file": "teaching/oe-grammar/lessons/0001-sound-change-and-reconstruction.html", "source_location": "2d. How Did Accent Work in PIE?", "weight": 1.0}, {"source": "lesson_0001_sound_change", "target": "pie_stop_system", "relation": "defines", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1.0, "source_file": "teaching/oe-grammar/lessons/0001-sound-change-and-reconstruction.html", "source_location": "2c. The Major PIE Stop Consonants", "weight": 1.0}, {"source": "lesson_0001_sound_change", "target": "palatalization", "relation": "defines", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1.0, "source_file": "teaching/oe-grammar/lessons/0001-sound-change-and-reconstruction.html", "source_location": "3c. Old English c/g Palatalization", "weight": 1.0}, {"source": "lesson_0001_sound_change", "target": "dotted_c_and_g", "relation": "defines", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1.0, "source_file": "teaching/oe-grammar/lessons/0001-sound-change-and-reconstruction.html", "source_location": "3c. Old English c/g Palatalization", "weight": 1.0}, {"source": "lesson_0001_sound_change", "target": "oe_dialects_late_layer", "relation": "defines", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1.0, "source_file": "teaching/oe-grammar/lessons/0001-sound-change-and-reconstruction.html", "source_location": "1b. Where the Old English Dialects Fit", "weight": 1.0}, {"source": "lesson_0001_sound_change", "target": "oe_fot_etymology", "relation": "defines", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1.0, "source_file": "teaching/oe-grammar/lessons/0001-sound-change-and-reconstruction.html", "source_location": "4a. Worked Walk-Backs from Old English", "weight": 1.0}, {"source": "lesson_0001_sound_change", "target": "oe_faeder_etymology", "relation": "defines", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1.0, "source_file": "teaching/oe-grammar/lessons/0001-sound-change-and-reconstruction.html", "source_location": "4a. Worked Walk-Backs from Old English", "weight": 1.0}, {"source": "lesson_0001_sound_change", "target": "oe_teon_etymology", "relation": "defines", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1.0, "source_file": "teaching/oe-grammar/lessons/0001-sound-change-and-reconstruction.html", "source_location": "4a. Worked Walk-Backs from Old English", "weight": 1.0}, {"source": "lesson_0001_sound_change", "target": "vowel_contraction", "relation": "defines", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1.0, "source_file": "teaching/oe-grammar/lessons/0001-sound-change-and-reconstruction.html", "source_location": "4a. OE t\u0113on", "weight": 1.0}, {"source": "grimms_law", "target": "proto_germanic", "relation": "precedes", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1.0, "source_file": "teaching/oe-grammar/lessons/0001-sound-change-and-reconstruction.html", "source_location": "1a. Branching Sketch", "weight": 1.0}, {"source": "grimms_law", "target": "verners_law", "relation": "precedes", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1.0, "source_file": "teaching/oe-grammar/lessons/0001-sound-change-and-reconstruction.html", "source_location": "2b. Why This Order?", "weight": 1.0}, {"source": "verners_law", "target": "germanic_fixed_initial_stress", "relation": "precedes", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score